A char is 1 byte, and an int and a float are 4 bytes each. What is the smallest value `sizeof(struct studentT)` can return for this struct?

```c
struct studentT {
    char name[64];
    int age;
    float gpa;
    int grad_yr;
};
```

The compiler treats each field as an offset from the start of the struct variable's memory. For the struct below, with 1-byte chars, 4-byte ints and no extra space between fields, at what byte offset does the `gpa` field start?

```c
struct studentT {
    char name[64];
    int age;
    float gpa;
    int grad_yr;
};
```

In main, `student.name` is "Ruth" and `student.age` is 17. What does this code print?

```c
int checkID(struct studentT s, int min_age) {
    int ret = 1;
    if (s.age < min_age) {
        ret = 0;
        s.age = min_age + 1;
    }
    printf("%s is %d years old\n", s.name, s.age);
    return ret;
}

/* in main: */
can_vote = checkID(student, 18);
if (can_vote) {
    printf("%s is %d years old and can vote.\n", student.name, student.age);
} else {
    printf("%s is only %d years old and cannot vote.\n", student.name, student.age);
}
```

What does this code print?

```c
void birthday(struct studentT s) {
    s.age = s.age + 1;
}

/* in main: */
student1.age = 20;
birthday(student1);
printf("%d\n", student1.age);
```
